1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,280
When a subsea well heats up, the conductor and the casings want to grow.

2
00:00:04,380 --> 00:00:06,866
If you hold the wellhead so it cannot move,

3
00:00:06,966 --> 00:00:09,633
something has to carry that blocked expansion.

4
00:00:09,733 --> 00:00:11,497
That force is the lock-in load.

5
00:00:12,017 --> 00:00:15,107
In this video we calculate it for two conductor sizes.

6
00:00:15,207 --> 00:00:17,175
A thirty inch with a one inch wall,

7
00:00:17,275 --> 00:00:20,365
and a thirty six inch with a one and a half inch wall.

8
00:00:20,465 --> 00:00:25,091
After one year of production, holding them down takes about nineteen and a half,

9
00:00:25,191 --> 00:00:26,745
and twenty four meganewtons.

10
00:00:26,845 --> 00:00:30,113
Roughly two thousand, and two and a half thousand tonnes.

11
00:00:30,650 --> 00:00:35,038
We will build that number step by step by hand, check it against the full model,

12
00:00:35,138 --> 00:00:37,732
and look at what the soil does, and does not do.

13
00:00:39,050 --> 00:00:42,180
Start with the forces on the wellhead once the well is hot.

14
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:46,342
The inner casings and the tubing heat up, and push up through their hangers.

15
00:00:46,883 --> 00:00:51,463
The conductor, the twenty inch surface casing and the cement between them are

16
00:00:51,563 --> 00:00:52,860
bonded into one column.

17
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:57,236
Near the seabed it heats up too, and pushes up on the wellhead housings.

18
00:00:57,783 --> 00:00:59,943
If the wellhead is not allowed to move,

19
00:01:00,043 --> 00:01:03,014
the restraint must push down with the sum of the two.

20
00:01:03,114 --> 00:01:04,462
That is the lock-in load.

21
00:01:04,983 --> 00:01:07,728
And the load path does not end at the restraint.

22
00:01:07,828 --> 00:01:12,469
The restraint has to be anchored, so the same force leaves through its anchor as

23
00:01:12,569 --> 00:01:12,884
uplift.

24
00:01:12,984 --> 00:01:17,447
Below the seabed, the column's compression is passed to the soil by friction,

25
00:01:17,547 --> 00:01:21,062
and at the conductor shoe, into the twenty inch and the rock.

26
00:01:22,383 --> 00:01:24,059
Both cases use the same well.

27
00:01:24,159 --> 00:01:28,162
A vertical oil producer in three hundred and fifty metres of water,

28
00:01:28,262 --> 00:01:31,653
with a twenty inch surface casing cemented to the seabed,

29
00:01:31,753 --> 00:01:33,367
and the usual inner strings.

30
00:01:34,517 --> 00:01:38,884
Option A is a thirty inch by one inch conductor in a thirty six inch hole,

31
00:01:38,984 --> 00:01:42,687
with five hundred and eighty eight square centimetres of steel.

32
00:01:42,787 --> 00:01:47,637
Option B is a thirty six inch by one and a half inch conductor in a forty two inch

33
00:01:47,737 --> 00:01:49,992
hole, with one thousand and forty nine.

34
00:01:50,092 --> 00:01:51,380
Almost twice the steel.

35
00:01:51,917 --> 00:01:55,796
The oil reaches the wellhead at about one hundred and eight degrees,

36
00:01:55,896 --> 00:01:58,547
a hundred and two above the seabed temperature.

37
00:01:58,647 --> 00:02:02,995
Just below the seabed, the thirty inch conductor warms by sixty one degrees,

38
00:02:03,095 --> 00:02:06,623
the thirty six inch by fifty five because it sits further out,

39
00:02:06,723 --> 00:02:08,730
and the twenty inch by seventy five.

40
00:02:08,830 --> 00:02:12,475
Without a heat transfer model, these are roughly zero point six,

41
00:02:12,575 --> 00:02:17,040
zero point five four, and zero point seven four of the fluid temperature rise.

42
00:02:18,383 --> 00:02:20,064
Now the hand calculation.

43
00:02:20,164 --> 00:02:25,692
For each bonded component, the force needed to stop it expanding is E, times A,

44
00:02:25,792 --> 00:02:27,615
times alpha, times delta T.

45
00:02:28,150 --> 00:02:33,204
For the thirty inch conductor, the steel area is zero point zero five eight eight

46
00:02:33,304 --> 00:02:34,095
square metres.

47
00:02:34,195 --> 00:02:36,640
Times two hundred and seven gigapascals,

48
00:02:36,740 --> 00:02:39,567
times twelve point four millionths per degree,

49
00:02:39,667 --> 00:02:43,193
that is one hundred and fifty one kilonewtons per degree.

50
00:02:43,293 --> 00:02:46,502
Times sixty one degrees, nine point two meganewtons.

51
00:02:47,050 --> 00:02:51,428
Do the same for the twenty inch and for both cement sheaths, and add them up.

52
00:02:51,528 --> 00:02:54,800
Sixteen point four meganewtons for the thirty inch system,

53
00:02:54,900 --> 00:02:57,766
and twenty three point two for the thirty six inch.

54
00:02:58,283 --> 00:03:01,083
Steel carries about eighty five percent of it.

55
00:03:01,183 --> 00:03:04,361
And the total depends on steel area and temperature,

56
00:03:04,461 --> 00:03:06,442
not on how long the conductor is.

57
00:03:06,542 --> 00:03:10,539
A longer pipe wants to grow more, but it is also a softer spring,

58
00:03:10,639 --> 00:03:12,178
so the length cancels out.

59
00:03:13,516 --> 00:03:15,614
The inner strings add their own push.

60
00:03:15,714 --> 00:03:19,238
Here, use the average temperature rise over each free length,

61
00:03:19,338 --> 00:03:23,456
from the hanger down to the top of cement, because it falls with depth.

62
00:03:23,983 --> 00:03:27,906
The thirteen and three eighths gives two point four meganewtons,

63
00:03:28,006 --> 00:03:32,433
the nine and five eighths one point six, and the tubing zero point five.

64
00:03:32,533 --> 00:03:37,526
Four and a half in total, or four point three after the small relief from helical

65
00:03:37,626 --> 00:03:38,091
buckling.

66
00:03:38,191 --> 00:03:40,292
It is the same for both conductors.

67
00:03:40,816 --> 00:03:45,173
Add it to the conductor system, and the hand estimate is twenty point nine

68
00:03:45,273 --> 00:03:49,992
meganewtons for the thirty inch, and twenty seven point seven for the thirty six

69
00:03:50,092 --> 00:03:50,293
inch.

70
00:03:50,393 --> 00:03:54,690
These are upper bounds, because every component is assumed fully blocked.

71
00:03:56,016 --> 00:03:57,774
So, does the soil hold it down?

72
00:03:57,874 --> 00:03:59,093
The model includes it.

73
00:03:59,193 --> 00:04:03,888
Every metre of the column sits on a soil spring that gives skin friction against

74
00:04:03,988 --> 00:04:04,727
the hole wall.

75
00:04:05,249 --> 00:04:08,882
But friction only acts where the pipe slides past the soil.

76
00:04:08,982 --> 00:04:12,424
A restrained column is held at the top by the restraint,

77
00:04:12,524 --> 00:04:15,714
and below about a hundred metres by cement and rock.

78
00:04:15,814 --> 00:04:18,877
In between, it moves only two to five millimetres.

79
00:04:18,977 --> 00:04:22,104
So the soil cannot take the load off the restraint.

80
00:04:22,649 --> 00:04:27,399
The little relief there is comes from the column pushing down into the soil below

81
00:04:27,499 --> 00:04:28,237
the restraint.

82
00:04:28,337 --> 00:04:31,410
Soft soil lets it do more of that, so the load drops.

83
00:04:31,510 --> 00:04:35,900
Stiff soil stops it, so the load climbs towards the hand calculation bound.

84
00:04:36,449 --> 00:04:37,520
Here is the effect.

85
00:04:37,620 --> 00:04:42,141
Make the soil ten times weaker, and the thirty inch lock-in load falls from

86
00:04:42,241 --> 00:04:44,852
nineteen and a half to fourteen meganewtons.

87
00:04:44,952 --> 00:04:48,733
Make it four times stronger, and it rises to twenty and a half.

88
00:04:48,833 --> 00:04:51,136
For a free wellhead it is the opposite.

89
00:04:51,236 --> 00:04:55,819
Weak soil lets it grow two hundred millimetres, stiff soil only thirty five.

90
00:04:56,349 --> 00:04:58,821
Where the soil really matters is elsewhere.

91
00:04:58,921 --> 00:05:01,273
Its friction capacity over the conductor,

92
00:05:01,373 --> 00:05:06,118
about twelve and fourteen and a half meganewtons, is less than the blocked force.

93
00:05:06,218 --> 00:05:10,424
So a free conductor slides, and grows about forty millimetres by itself.

94
00:05:10,524 --> 00:05:14,910
And the restraint's own anchor has to hold the full lock-in load in uplift,

95
00:05:15,010 --> 00:05:15,927
in the same soil.

96
00:05:17,249 --> 00:05:21,461
The full model gives nineteen and a half meganewtons for the thirty inch,

97
00:05:21,561 --> 00:05:24,414
and twenty four point one for the thirty six inch.

98
00:05:24,514 --> 00:05:27,309
Seven and thirteen percent below the hand bounds.

99
00:05:27,409 --> 00:05:31,857
The conductor steel is the biggest share, and the only one that changes much.

100
00:05:31,957 --> 00:05:35,105
Eight and a half, against twelve point six meganewtons.

101
00:05:35,649 --> 00:05:38,346
A restraint that gives a little relieves a lot.

102
00:05:38,446 --> 00:05:42,750
Halving the growth still costs about eight and a half to nine meganewtons,

103
00:05:42,850 --> 00:05:47,154
and every millimetre held back costs a third, to almost half a meganewton.

104
00:05:47,683 --> 00:05:50,655
And the load keeps creeping up as the rock warms.

105
00:05:50,755 --> 00:05:53,853
Twelve to fourteen meganewtons after the first day,

106
00:05:53,953 --> 00:05:56,613
twenty one and twenty seven after ten years.

107
00:05:56,713 --> 00:05:59,058
So design for end of life temperatures.

108
00:05:59,158 --> 00:06:03,322
There, the hand bounds are twenty three, and thirty one meganewtons.

109
00:06:04,649 --> 00:06:06,838
A few more effects change the picture.

110
00:06:06,938 --> 00:06:10,934
The lock-in load comes on top of the loads already in the conductor,

111
00:06:11,034 --> 00:06:15,873
from the wellhead and tree, the hanging casings, and riser loads during workovers.

112
00:06:15,973 --> 00:06:20,751
And every shut in and start up cycles it, so it is a fatigue load, not a one off.

113
00:06:21,283 --> 00:06:23,314
It scales roughly with temperature.

114
00:06:23,414 --> 00:06:28,185
About zero point two meganewtons for every degree of fluid temperature rise with

115
00:06:28,285 --> 00:06:32,204
the thirty inch, and zero point two four with the thirty six inch.

116
00:06:32,749 --> 00:06:35,207
And the highest stress is not at the seabed.

117
00:06:35,307 --> 00:06:39,740
At the conductor shoe, the conductor hands its compression to the twenty inch.

118
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:44,332
There the twenty inch locally reaches about three hundred and eight megapascals

119
00:06:44,432 --> 00:06:49,156
with the thirty inch conductor, and three hundred and fifty two with the thirty six

120
00:06:49,256 --> 00:06:49,446
inch.

121
00:06:49,546 --> 00:06:52,875
Eighty one, and ninety three percent of K fifty five yield.

122
00:06:54,216 --> 00:06:57,238
So the design checks are the restraint and its anchor,

123
00:06:57,338 --> 00:06:59,551
every component the load passes through,

124
00:06:59,651 --> 00:07:03,829
the conductor and the twenty inch at the seabed and at the conductor shoe,

125
00:07:03,929 --> 00:07:08,397
the thirteen and three eighths hanger lockdown, and fatigue for every start up.

126
00:07:08,916 --> 00:07:11,540
And to reduce the load: allow some movement,

127
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:14,822
or engage the restraint only after the first heat up.

128
00:07:14,922 --> 00:07:17,608
Reduce the temperature with insulated tubing.

129
00:07:17,708 --> 00:07:20,766
Choose the conductor with the lock-in load in mind.

130
00:07:20,866 --> 00:07:24,233
And make sure the twenty inch is cemented to the seabed.

131
00:07:24,783 --> 00:07:25,229
In short.

132
00:07:25,329 --> 00:07:30,266
The lock-in load is the blocked thermal force of everything bonded at the wellhead,

133
00:07:30,366 --> 00:07:32,390
plus the push of the inner strings.

134
00:07:32,490 --> 00:07:34,635
Steel area times temperature sets it.

135
00:07:34,735 --> 00:07:36,517
The soil does not hold it down.

136
00:07:36,617 --> 00:07:39,612
It only decides how far a free wellhead would grow,

137
00:07:39,712 --> 00:07:42,039
and how well your restraint is anchored.
